course: dictionary
lesson: 13514
title: Judging fairly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how something is done. Impartially is an adverb, so it describes an action. It means in a fair way, without favouring anyone or taking sides. «The referee blew the whistle impartially, so neither team could complain.» The referee treated both teams the same, so the word tells you the decisions were fair.
explain: Look for clues in the word itself. Partial means favouring one side, and im- means not, so impartial means not favouring one side. Ask yourself: does this person pick a favourite? If not, they are acting impartially. «The judge listened impartially to both children before deciding who had won.»
explain: Impartially is often used for people whose job is to decide: referees, judges, teachers and reporters. «The reporter wrote about both parties impartially, giving each side the same space.» To check your meaning, swap in "fairly, without taking sides". If the sentence still makes sense, you have understood it.
fact: Impartially means in a fair way, without favouring anyone or taking sides.
fact: Impartially is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
fact: The formal meaning is judging only on facts and rules, not on who people are.
fact: Common phrases include act impartially, judge impartially and report impartially.
fact: The prefix im- means "not", and partial means favouring one side, so impartial means not favouring one side.
exercise: «The teacher marked every test impartially, not caring whose name was on it.» What does impartially mean here? | *In a fair way, without favouring anyone | In an angry way, after an argument | In a quick way, to finish early | In a loud way, so everyone can hear
exercise: «The judge must listen impartially to both sides before deciding.» What does impartially suggest about the judge? | *Treats both sides the same, with no favourite | Likes one side more than the other | Listens only to the loudest person | Falls asleep during the case
exercise: Amir says impartially means quickly. What clue shows he is wrong? | It is a long word, so it must mean slowly | *Impartially means fairly, without taking sides, not speed | Impartially means quickly and fairly | It sounds like impossible
test: «The referee judged every goal impartially.» What does this tell you about the referee? | *He was fair, with no favourite | He was taking his own team's side | He was running after the ball | He changed the rules during the game
test: Which phrase best matches "report impartially"? | *Tell both sides of the story fairly | Tell it to the most important person | Tell only the most exciting part | Write it as a poem
exam: Which sentence uses impartially correctly? | Lucy picked her best friend as the winner, impartially | Dad ate his dinner impartially | Mia's dog barked impartially at the postman | *The judge heard both families and decided the case impartially
